Jimmy Lai rose from poverty to become one of Hong Kong’s most successful entrepreneurs. Today, he is one of the world’s most prominent political prisoners.

For decades, the world believed Hong Kong’s freedoms would endure. Lai’s case is a stark reminder of how those promises have been abandoned and a test of whether mercy, human dignity, and basic freedoms still have a place under the Chinese Communist Party’s rule.

@SteveYates is a Senior Research Fellow for China and National Security Policy
